{"task_id": "Mbpp/245", "solution": "def max_sum(arr: list[int]) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the maximum sum of a bitonic subsequence for a given array.\n    A sequence is bitonic if it is first increasing and then decreasing.\n    \"\"\"\n    n = len(arr)\n    if n == 0:\n        return 0\n\n    # _calculate_lis_sums computes the maximum sum of an increasing subsequence\n    # ending at each position.\n    def _calculate_lis_sums(current_arr: list[int]) -> list[int]:\n        current_n = len(current_arr)\n        if current_n == 0:\n            return []\n        dp = list(current_arr)\n        for i in range(current_n):\n            for j in range(i):\n                if current_arr[i] > current_arr[j]:\n                    dp[i] = max(dp[i], dp[j] + current_arr[i])\n        return dp\n\n    # _calculate_lds_sums computes the maximum sum of a decreasing subsequence\n    # starting at each position (elements to its right).\n    def _calculate_lds_sums(current_arr: list[int]) -> list[int]:\n        current_n = len(current_arr)\n        if current_n == 0:\n            return []\n        dp = list(current_arr)\n        # Iterate from right to left\n        for i in range(current_n - 1, -1, -1):\n            # Compare with elements to its right\n            for j in range(i + 1, current_n):\n                if current_arr[i] > current_arr[j]:\n                    dp[i] = max(dp[i], dp[j] + current_arr[i])\n        return dp\n\n    # Calculate LIS sums for the original array (increasing part)\n    lis_sums = _calculate_lis_sums(arr)\n\n    # Calculate LDS sums for the original array (decreasing part)\n    lds_sums = _calculate_lds_sums(arr)\n\n    max_bitonic_sum = 0\n    if n > 0:\n        # Initialize max_bitonic_sum with a single element, which is a valid bitonic sequence\n        # This handles cases where all elements are negative or a sequence is purely increasing/decreasing.\n        max_bitonic_sum = arr[0] \n        # For small arrays (e.g., [1]), lis_sums[0] + lds_sums[0] - arr[0] is arr[0].\n        # However, to be robust for any value, including negatives,\n        # it's better to ensure max_bitonic_sum is at least the max of individual elements,\n        # which will be covered by the loop as single elements are also counted in lis/lds.\n        # So starting with 0 and taking max is usually fine for non-negative sums, \n        # or float('-inf') if negative sums are important and we want the largest negative.\n        # Given the problem context and assert, starting with 0 is acceptable.\n\n    for i in range(n):\n        # A bitonic subsequence with arr[i] as peak has a sum of lis_sums[i] + lds_sums[i] - arr[i]\n        # (arr[i] is counted in both LIS and LDS sums).\n        current_sum = lis_sums[i] + lds_sums[i] - arr[i]\n        max_bitonic_sum = max(max_bitonic_sum, current_sum)\n\n    return max_bitonic_sum"}
