{"task_id": "Mbpp/589", "solution": "def perfect_squares(lower: int, upper: int) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds and returns a sorted list of perfect squares within an inclusive range.\n    It calculates squares of integers from ceil(sqrt(max(1, lower))) to floor(sqrt(upper)).\n    \"\"\"\n    import math # math module is required for sqrt, ceil, floor\n\n    perfect_squares_list = []\n\n    # Perfect squares are non-negative. If upper is negative, there are no perfect squares.\n    if upper < 0:\n        return []\n\n    # Calculate the starting integer whose square is >= lower.\n    # We use max(1, lower) because perfect squares typically refer to positive integers,\n    # and 0 is not included in the example range (1, 30).\n    start_root = math.ceil(math.sqrt(max(1, lower)))\n\n    # Calculate the ending integer whose square is <= upper.\n    end_root = math.floor(math.sqrt(upper))\n\n    # Iterate through potential integer roots and add their squares if they fall within the range.\n    for i in range(start_root, end_root + 1):\n        square = i * i\n        if lower <= square <= upper:\n            perfect_squares_list.append(square)\n\n    return perfect_squares_list"}
