{"task_id": "Mbpp/605", "solution": "import math\ndef prime_num(number: int) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if the given integer is a prime number using an optimized trial division method.\n    Returns True if the number is prime, False otherwise.\n    \"\"\"\n    if number <= 1:\n        return False\n    if number == 2 or number == 3:\n        return True\n    if number % 2 == 0 or number % 3 == 0:\n        return False\n    \n    i = 5\n    # Iterate up to the square root of the number. Primes greater than 3 can be written as 6k +/- 1.\n    while i * i <= number:\n        if number % i == 0 or number % (i + 2) == 0:\n            return False\n        i += 6\n    \n    return True"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/607", "solution": "import re\ndef find_literals(target_string: str, pattern: str) -> tuple[str, int, int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Searches a target string for a regex pattern and returns the first matching substring,\n    its start index, and its end index.\n    \"\"\"\n    match = re.search(pattern, target_string)\n    if match:\n        return match.group(0), match.start(), match.end()\n    # Assuming a match will always be found based on problem description and example.\n    # If no match is found, a more robust implementation might raise an error or return None.\n    # For this problem, we will proceed assuming a match object is always returned by re.search.\n    # This implies the problem context guarantees a match for the given inputs.\n    # To satisfy the return type hint given the example, we need to return something\n    # that fits the tuple structure. Since the example always finds a match,\n    # we don't need to explicitly handle a non-match scenario for now.\n    # If no match is found, and the type hint must be satisfied, this part needs explicit definition.\n    # However, for a complete implementation adhering strictly to the example,\n    # the 'if match' path is sufficient.\n    return \"\", -1, -1 # Default for no match if required, but problem implies a match exists."}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/603", "solution": "def get_ludic(n: int) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"Computes all Ludic numbers less than or equal to n using the sieve method.\"\"\"\n    if n <= 0:\n        return []\n\n    ludic_numbers = [1]\n    # Initialize candidates with numbers from 2 up to n.\n    # These are the numbers from which subsequent Ludic numbers will be picked and sieved.\n    candidates = list(range(2, n + 1))\n\n    while candidates:\n        # The first number in the current list of candidates is the next Ludic number.\n        current_ludic = candidates[0]\n        ludic_numbers.append(current_ludic)\n\n        # Create a new list for the next iteration's candidates.\n        new_candidates = []\n        # Keep track of the 1-based position in the sequence being sieved.\n        # This position is relative to the `candidates` list *after* `current_ludic` has been removed (conceptually).\n        position_in_sieve = 1\n\n        # Iterate through the candidates list, starting from the second element (index 1),\n        # as the first element (`current_ludic`) has already been chosen.\n        for i in range(1, len(candidates)):\n            # If the current element's position is not a multiple of `current_ludic`, it is kept.\n            if position_in_sieve % current_ludic != 0:\n                new_candidates.append(candidates[i])\n            position_in_sieve += 1\n        \n        candidates = new_candidates\n\n    return ludic_numbers"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/614", "solution": "def cummulative_sum(tuple_list: list[tuple[int | float, ...]]) -> int | float:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the cumulative sum of all numerical values present in a list of tuples.\n\n    Args:\n        tuple_list: A list where each element is a tuple containing numerical values.\n\n    Returns:\n        A single numerical value representing the total sum.\n    \"\"\"\n    total = 0\n    for tup in tuple_list:\n        for value in tup:\n            total += value\n    return total"}
