{"task_id": "Mbpp/608", "solution": "def bell_Number(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"Calculates the nth Bell number.\"\"\"\n    if n == 0:\n        return 1\n\n    # `stirling_row[k]` will store Stirling numbers of the second kind S(i, k)\n    # for the current row `i` being computed.\n    # It's initialized to represent S(0, k), where S(0, 0) = 1 and others are 0.\n    stirling_row = [0] * (n + 1)\n    stirling_row[0] = 1  # Base case: S(0, 0) = 1\n\n    # Iterate from i = 1 to n to calculate S(i, k) for each i.\n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        # To compute S(i, k) using the recurrence S(i, k) = S(i-1, k-1) + k * S(i-1, k),\n        # we iterate `k` downwards. This ensures that `stirling_row[k-1]` and\n        # `stirling_row[k]` still hold values from the *previous* row (i-1) before being updated\n        # for the current row `i`.\n        for k in range(i, 0, -1):\n            stirling_row[k] = stirling_row[k - 1] + k * stirling_row[k]\n        \n        # Base case for Stirling numbers: S(i, 0) is always 0 for i > 0.\n        # So, stirling_row[0] should be reset to 0 for the current row `i`.\n        stirling_row[0] = 0\n\n    # The nth Bell number B_n is the sum of S(n, k) for k from 0 to n.\n    # At this point, `stirling_row` contains S(n, k) for k = 0 to n.\n    return sum(stirling_row)"}
